# Day 52

**35km (on canoe) / 6 hours**

*Whakahoro to John Coull campsite*

Yay we're finally going! This was much anticipated and it felt similar to the biking section: quicker and a change up but also more mentally tiring. The river was still quite high from the previous heavy rain but was much more manageable; this meant it would be quicker to get down the river. 

We started from the landing near the Whakahoro campsite where the canoes were unloaded and the gear packed into barrels and bags which was a much trickier ordeal than I had anticipated. Turns out I had a lot of gear that was oddly shaped and didn't quite fit the way I wanted but eventually this was able to be sorted but it did mean our canoe was the final one to leave.

![The river looking brown and high from the first campsite where we had lunch](./river.avif)

I was at the front of the canoe which is mainly responsible for paddling and driving it forward whereas the guy at the back handles steering. This is nice but I think the way I tend to paddle disrupts a steady consistent rhythm that night be built up so I need to work on improving that. I did a similar thing on the kayak from Puhoi but there it was just me so not as much of an issue. 

The river itself was incredible as expected, the beautiful bush alongside the grand river was scintillating. The banks were also nice, some rocky, some grassy. There were plenty of ducks and a few ducklings swimming around. Cool secret little streams and waterfalls littered the sides of the river as well. It meant that whenever there was a break from paddling there was always something great to see.

![Another view of the river from the canoe](./river-2.avif)

The camaraderie from the group was also fantastic. Since we were getting on after a period where they weren't sending people it was a large group and that made it tricky to park the canoes in congested areas but everyone made room or helped each other out so no one was stuck. Also people played with the water a bit and it was all quite fun. The jetboats that came up were also relatively calm about it so as to avoid causing large waves for us.

![A pretty swag cave along the path](./cave.avif)

Overall it was a fantastic day although like the cycling when I lay down in my tent at the end of the day my head is spinning a little. I think it's the up and down movement but it's not a real problem, just a little strange. Looking forward to the next few days which should have other interesting sections along the way.